//! member: true
//!
//! std.str — the documented API surface of the language's `str`: an
//! immutable, guaranteed-valid UTF-8 slice, two machine words wide (D24).
//! Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **What is the language's and what is std's** (the API-CONVENTIONS §8
//! question, asked again for strings). The language owns:
//!
//! - every literal — every string literal is an f-string, `{x}`
//!   interpolates, `{{`/`}}` escape, `"""` dedents by the closing
//!   delimiter's column, `r"…"` is raw (`[gram.lex.str]`, D26/X9);
//! - `s.len` — the length in BYTES, O(1) (D25);
//! - `s[a..b]` — zero-copy byte-offset slicing, checked: an out-of-range
//!   offset OR an offset inside a code point traps `bounds`
//!   (`[conf.trap.set]`), deterministically, in every profile;
//! - the relational family — `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `<=>` are
//!   byte-lexicographic over the UTF-8 bytes (`[mem.str.order]`, the
//!   ruling this repo's F-0006 asked for);
//! - the builtin method set this file delegates to, which is now the whole
//!   s37 set: `is_empty`, `get`, `bytes`, `contains`, `starts_with`,
//!   `ends_with`, `find`, `rfind`, `count`, `split`, `words`, `each_word`, `words_count`, `lines`,
//!   `trim`, `trim_start`, `trim_end`, `lower`, `upper`, `strip_prefix`,
//!   `strip_suffix`, `repeat`, `replace`.
//!
//! Everything else is std's — this file. Functions take the string as
//! their first parameter with the bare `read` mode, because `str` is
//! immutable and nothing here mutates: the builder is `std.strbuf`.
//!
//! **NO CHAR INDEXING, and no negative indices** (D25). There is no
//! `s[i]`: a byte offset is the one honest currency, so `find` returns
//! offsets and `char_offsets` yields them. To reach the first code point,
//! slice (`s[..n]`) or ask `get` for a range it may refuse. `^n`
//! end-relative indexing (`s[..^1]`) is the language's spelling for the
//! other end and does not resolve in either implementation at this pin
//! (F-0018's last living clause); there is deliberately no std function
//! that papers over it.
//!
//! **THE BOUNDARY PRIMITIVE LANDED, AND THIS MODULE IS WHAT IT BOUGHT.**
//! For five sprints fifteen of this module's functions were reviewed
//! contracts with no bodies, because every position-taking string
//! algorithm needs a way to ask "is offset k a code-point boundary?" and
//! the only way to ask was to slice at k — which TRAPS when the answer is
//! no, uncatchably (D30, no unwinding). s37 shipped the recoverable slice
//! `s.get(a..b) -> str ! {none}` (`[mem.str.get]`) plus `find`/`rfind`/
//! `split`/`count`/`ends_with`/`strip_suffix`/`replace` and the byte view
//! `s.bytes() -> List[int]`, and all three lanes execute them (wolfc at
//! s37, lupin at 0.1.5, native at #40). Twelve of the fifteen are code
//! below. What remains is exactly what needs a type wolf does not have:
//!
//! ```text
//! chars(s: str) -> List[(int, char)]
//!     Code points with their byte offsets. Blocked twice over: no `char`
//!     type exists, and wolfc does not type a `List` of tuples at all
//!     (`List[(int, int)]` is `unsupported`), so even the interim
//!     currency cannot carry the pair. `char_offsets` below is the offset
//!     half and `code_points` the value half; the day `char` lands they
//!     fuse into this one function and the `Iter` protocol (F-0008)
//!     decides whether it yields or allocates.
//! to_list_chars(s: str) -> List[char]
//!     The code points alone. `code_points` below IS this function in
//!     `std.unicode`'s interim currency — a Unicode scalar value as
//!     `int` — and the flip is a signature change with an identical body.
//! graphemes(s: str) -> List[str]
//!     Extended grapheme clusters, as slices of `s`. A std call and never
//!     the default unit (D24). Needs segmentation TABLES, which is the
//!     filed budget decision (F-0018) and not a primitive gap: nothing
//!     about this pin's `str` brings it closer.
//! ```
//!
//! **THE BYTE VIEW IS A VIEW NOW, AND FOUR BODIES HERE ARE WRITTEN AGAINST
//! THAT** (s77 / wolf-lang#80, the sc12 pin). `s.bytes()` is the receiver's
//! own pointer and length — bit-identical to the `str` and to every zero-copy
//! subslice `trim`/`split`/`get` already returned. Lowering reads that pair
//! wherever the call is CONSUMED, so a byte walk emits no call and no
//! allocation; `s[a..b]` and `s.get(a..b)` stopped calling the runtime in
//! the same wave.
//!
//! **std used TWO of those consuming positions for two sprints because the
//! other five cost a lane, and at the sc12 pin the constraint is GONE**
//! (F-0071 CLOSED by s88's #85, "a temporary can be read from" —
//! re-measured shape by shape: `s.bytes()[i]`, `.get(i)`, `.first()`,
//! `.last()` and `.count()` all execute on wolfc's checked tier now, where
//! every one of them was `unsupported` at `mem`). The walks below did not
//! change back, and that is a decision rather than an oversight: the
//! one-pass state machine `code_points` became is SHORTER than the
//! random-access walk it replaced, and a constraint that forced a better
//! algorithm is not one to undo the day it lifts. What the closure buys is
//! that a future body may index a view without paying a lane for it. The
//! rule it was an instance of stands unchanged: a shape that costs a lane
//! is not a shape std writes (§14's honesty, applied to an optimization
//! rather than to a capability). lupin still has no `first` on a `List`,
//! measured at this pin, so that one position is still not portable.
//!
//! What changed for this module is four bodies and no signature.
//! `char_count`, `char_offsets`, `code_points` and `is_ascii` were written
//! in sc09 as `let bs = s.bytes()` plus a walk over `bs`, which was the only
//! shape available when the builtin materialized a `List[int]` per call.
//! Nothing a caller can see moved: same answers, same rows, same lanes, and
//! `tests/str/code_point_view.lu` is what says so.
//!
//! **The boundary of the win is a function boundary**, and it is worth
//! stating where a reader will meet it: every position that is NOT
//! consuming — a `let` binding, an argument, a return — materializes
//! exactly as before. So `str.bytes(s)` below still allocates (it returns
//! the list), `std.bytes`' functions still take a materialized `List[int]`,
//! and a std function cannot pass a view to a helper. The win lives inside
//! one body today, which is why this sprint's work was rewriting bodies and
//! not signatures.
//!
//! **THE EMPTY NEEDLE IS RULED, AND STD'S GUARDS ARE GONE** (`[mem.str.empty]`,
//! wolf-lang#56, F-0055 CLOSED at the sc11 pin). For three sprints
//! `count("")`, `split("")` and `replace(s, "", …)` were undefined and the
//! three lanes disagreed — two refused the call as `unsupported`, the native
//! runtime answered — so six functions here guarded the empty argument and
//! two of them TRAPPED, which was std stating a contract the toolchain had
//! not stated. s71 states it: an empty needle counts 0, splits to one whole
//! piece, and replaces nothing (identity). Measured on all three lanes at
//! this pin, so `count`, `split` and `replace` simply delegate now, and the
//! functions std writes itself agree with the ruling by construction:
//! `splitn` answers the one whole piece, `rsplit` inherits `split`'s,
//! `replacen` is the identity. Nothing in this module traps on an empty
//! argument any more. The `find`-defined family (`split_once`,
//! `rsplit_once`, `find_all`) inherits `find`'s answer instead, and says so
//! on itself.
//!
//! **PADDING LIVES IN `std.fmt`** (§11: the format spec is a spelling and
//! std owns the operations). `pad_left`, `pad_right`, `center`, their
//! `_with` fill variants and `with_sign` are `std.fmt`'s, not this
//! module's — one concept, one name, one home (§1). This module has no
//! width family and will not grow one; when display width (East Asian
//! Width plus graphemes) becomes computable it arrives under its own name
//! in the module that owns the tables.
//!
//! **THE PARSE FAMILY MOVED (sc05).** `parse_int` and `parse_float` were
//! shipped here by sc03 because this is where the boundary-safe probe was
//! proven; the sprint that owns PRINTING numbers owns the whole
//! number↔text boundary, so they now live in `std.fmt` and
//! `std.fmt.decimal` beside `to_str_base` and `to_str`, where a reader can
//! see the two directions as inverses and a test can hold them to it. Two
//! behaviours improved in the move: a radix outside 2..36 raises `base`
//! rather than `parse`, and `parse_float` is correctly rounded rather
//! than "honest but approximate". Nothing in `std.str` calls them, so
//! this module no longer carries a digit table at all.
//!
//! **The unicode posture, written down** (the sprint's filed decision).
//! What ships in v1: UTF-8 validity as a type invariant (the language's),
//! byte-offset slicing that refuses to split a code point (the language's),
//! and ASCII-true classification named as such (`std.unicode`, over the
//! `int` code points `code_points` now produces). What is NOT here and is
//! a budget decision filed upstream, not a silent gap: category tables,
//! full case mapping, normalization, and grapheme segmentation data. The
//! evidence: the implementations' `lower`/`upper` already perform SIMPLE
//! UNICODE case mapping (`"ÉA".lower()` is `"éa"`, observed on every
//! lane), which is MORE than the sprint contract promised and means the
//! tables are already being carried somewhere — so the decision to make
//! is where they live and who pays for them, not whether to start
//! (F-0018, with `std.unicode`'s posture).

use std.strbuf

/// The length of `s` in BYTES, O(1) — the honest unit (D25). This is the
/// documented byte-length test the sprint asks for: a two-byte code point
/// has length 2, and the number of CODE POINTS is a different question
/// that `char_count` answers. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.len("wolf") == 4
/// str.len("é") == 2
/// str.len("🐺") == 4
/// str.len("") == 0
/// ```
pub fn len(s: str) -> int {
    s.len
}

/// Whether `s` holds no bytes. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.is_empty("") == true
/// str.is_empty("wolf") == false
/// ```
pub fn is_empty(s: str) -> bool {
    s.is_empty()
}

/// The bytes in `[from, to)` — the RECOVERABLE slice, and the primitive
/// the rest of this module is written over (`[mem.str.get]`, D25's
/// anti-landmine form). `none` when the range is out of order, out of
/// range, or would split a code point: the one function that ANSWERS the
/// boundary question where `s[from..to]` faults on it. Never traps.
///
/// Spelled with two offsets rather than a range because a range value
/// crossing a std function boundary is not something every lane types
/// (F-0030); the builtin takes the range and this wrapper builds it.
///
/// Use `get` when the offsets are DATA (a caller's input, an offset from
/// another source) and `s[from..to]` when they are yours by construction —
/// an offset `find` returned is a boundary and a trap there would be a
/// bug, not a fault.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (str.get("héllo", 0, 3) else "?") == "hé"
/// (str.get("héllo", 0, 2) else "?") == "?"
/// (str.get("héllo", 1, 2) else "?") == "?"
/// (str.get("héllo", 0, 99) else "?") == "?"
/// (str.get("héllo", 3, 1) else "?") == "?"
/// (str.get("héllo", 0, 0) else "?") == ""
/// ```
pub fn get(s: str, from: int, to: int) -> str ! {none} {
    s.get(from..to)?
}

/// The raw bytes of `s`, in order, as the 0..255 element values §11's
/// interim byte currency uses — `std.bytes` is the module that operates on
/// them and `bytes.from_str` is the same view from that side. Total: no
/// error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// **This function allocates and the language's `s.bytes()` no longer
/// does, and the difference is the whole s77 story.** A byte view is the
/// receiver's own pointer and length, read in place wherever it is
/// CONSUMED — `for b in s.bytes()`, `s.bytes()[i]`, `s.bytes().len` and the
/// small query family — and materialized in every other position, which
/// includes being RETURNED from a function like this one. So this wrapper
/// is the honest `to_`-grade copy its name reserves the right to be (§1),
/// and the fast path is the language's own spelling inside one body. std's
/// own walks (`char_count`, `char_offsets`, `code_points`, `is_ascii`)
/// stopped calling this function in sc12 for exactly that reason; a caller
/// who wants the list — to keep it, to pass it to `std.bytes`, to hold it
/// past `s` — still wants this.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var n = 0
/// for b in str.bytes("hé") { n = n + b }
/// n = n + 0
/// n == 468
/// ```
pub fn bytes(s: str) -> List[int] {
    s.bytes()
}

/// Whether `needle` occurs anywhere in `s`, as a byte sequence. The empty
/// needle occurs in every string, including the empty one. Total: no error
/// row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.contains("the wolf runs", "wolf") == true
/// str.contains("the wolf runs", "moon") == false
/// str.contains("", "") == true
/// ```
pub fn contains(s: str, needle: str) -> bool {
    s.contains(needle)
}

/// The byte offset of the FIRST occurrence of `needle` in `s`; `none` when
/// it does not occur. The empty needle occurs at 0. Total otherwise: a
/// needle longer than `s` is a miss, not a fault, and no input traps.
///
/// The offset is a code-point boundary of `s` by construction, so slicing
/// at it is safe — which is what makes `split_once`, `find_all`,
/// `replacen` and `splitn` writable below.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (str.find("the wolf runs", "wolf") else 0 - 1) == 4
/// (str.find("the wolf runs", "moon") else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// (str.find("héllo", "l") else 0 - 1) == 3
/// (str.find("wolf", "") else 0 - 1) == 0
/// ```
pub fn find(s: str, needle: str) -> int ! {none} {
    s.find(needle)?
}

/// The byte offset of the LAST occurrence of `needle` in `s`; `none` when
/// it does not occur. The empty needle occurs at `len(s)` — the end, which
/// is what "last" means for something that occurs everywhere. Total
/// otherwise: no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (str.rfind("a/b/c", "/") else 0 - 1) == 3
/// (str.rfind("a/b/c", "x") else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// (str.rfind("héllo", "l") else 0 - 1) == 4
/// (str.rfind("wolf", "") else 0 - 1) == 4
/// ```
pub fn rfind(s: str, needle: str) -> int ! {none} {
    s.rfind(needle)?
}

/// The byte offsets of every NON-OVERLAPPING occurrence of `needle`, left
/// to right — the find-all family's one member, and the plural of `find`
/// rather than a new vocabulary. `count(s, needle)` is its length.
///
/// The empty needle yields no offsets, for `count`'s reason — `count` is
/// this function's length and the ruled count of nothing is 0
/// (`[mem.str.empty]`). The guard is also what makes the loop terminate:
/// `find("")` is 0 at every step, so a body that advanced by the needle's
/// length would not advance at all. Allocates the list. Total: no error
/// row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var n = 0
/// for at in str.find_all("a,b,c", ",") { n = n + at }
/// n = n + 0
/// n == 4
/// ```
pub fn find_all(s: str, needle: str) -> List[int] {
    var out = List[int]()
    if needle.is_empty() {
        return out
    }
    var base = 0
    var rest = s
    var more = true
    while more {
        let at = rest.find(needle) else 0 - 1
        if at < 0 {
            more = false
        } else {
            (mut out).push(base + at)
            let step = at + needle.len
            rest = rest[step..]
            base = base + step
        }
    }
    out
}

/// The number of NON-OVERLAPPING occurrences of `needle` in `s`, scanning
/// left to right — `"aaa"` contains one `"aa"`, not two. The empty needle
/// counts 0, not `len + 1`: a count of nothing is nothing, which was this
/// module's reviewed contract and is now the language's ruling
/// (`[mem.str.empty]`), so the guard std carried for three sprints is gone
/// and the delegation says the same thing. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.count("a,b,c", ",") == 2
/// str.count("aaa", "aa") == 1
/// str.count("wolf", "z") == 0
/// str.count("wolf", "") == 0
/// ```
pub fn count(s: str, needle: str) -> int {
    s.count(needle)
}

/// Whether `s` begins with `prefix`. The empty prefix always matches.
/// Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// When it answers true, `len(prefix)` is a code-point boundary of `s` —
/// the property sc03 built this module's first bodies on, kept here
/// because it is still the cheapest way to reason about an offset.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.starts_with("wolf-lang", "wolf") == true
/// str.starts_with("wolf-lang", "lang") == false
/// str.starts_with("🐺!", "🐺") == true
/// ```
pub fn starts_with(s: str, prefix: str) -> bool {
    s.starts_with(prefix)
}

/// Whether `s` ends with `suffix`. The empty suffix always matches.
/// Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.ends_with("wolf.lu", ".lu") == true
/// str.ends_with("wolf.lu", ".rs") == false
/// str.ends_with("a🐺", "🐺") == true
/// str.ends_with("wolf", "") == true
/// ```
pub fn ends_with(s: str, suffix: str) -> bool {
    s.ends_with(suffix)
}

/// Whether `s` begins with ANY of `prefixes` — the set form of
/// `starts_with`, for the switch a parser writes over a keyword list. An
/// empty list matches nothing; an empty prefix IN the list matches
/// everything, exactly as `starts_with` does. Total: no error row, no
/// traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var ps = List[str]()
/// (mut ps).push("http://")
/// (mut ps).push("https://")
/// str.starts_with_any("https://wolf.lang", ps) == true
/// str.starts_with_any("ftp://wolf.lang", ps) == false
/// ```
pub fn starts_with_any(s: str, prefixes: List[str]) -> bool {
    let n = prefixes.len
    var i = 0
    while i < n {
        let p = prefixes[i]
        if s.starts_with(p) {
            return true
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    false
}

/// Whether `s` ends with ANY of `suffixes` — the set form of `ends_with`,
/// for the extension test a file walker writes. An empty list matches
/// nothing. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var ss = List[str]()
/// (mut ss).push(".lu")
/// (mut ss).push(".wolfi")
/// str.ends_with_any("main.wolfi", ss) == true
/// str.ends_with_any("main.rs", ss) == false
/// ```
pub fn ends_with_any(s: str, suffixes: List[str]) -> bool {
    let n = suffixes.len
    var i = 0
    while i < n {
        let x = suffixes[i]
        if s.ends_with(x) {
            return true
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    false
}

/// `s` without a leading `prefix`; `none` when `s` does not start with it
/// — the absence row rather than `s` unchanged, so the caller can tell the
/// two apart (§2). Consumed by `else` or `?`. No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (str.strip_prefix("wolf-lang", "wolf-") else "?") == "lang"
/// (str.strip_prefix("wolf-lang", "lang") else "?") == "?"
/// (str.strip_prefix("wolf", "") else "?") == "wolf"
/// ```
pub fn strip_prefix(s: str, prefix: str) -> str ! {none} {
    s.strip_prefix(prefix)?
}

/// `s` without a trailing `suffix`; `none` when it is not there — the
/// absence row for `strip_prefix`'s reason (§2). Consumed by `else` or
/// `?`. No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (str.strip_suffix("main.lu", ".lu") else "?") == "main"
/// (str.strip_suffix("main.lu", ".rs") else "?") == "?"
/// (str.strip_suffix("wolf", "") else "?") == "wolf"
/// ```
pub fn strip_suffix(s: str, suffix: str) -> str ! {none} {
    s.strip_suffix(suffix)?
}

/// Every piece of `s` between occurrences of `sep`, in order, keeping
/// empty pieces: `split("a,,b", ",")` is three pieces and `split("", ",")`
/// is one empty piece. The pieces are slices of `s`; the list allocates.
///
/// An empty `sep` yields ONE piece — the whole of `s` — which is the
/// language's ruling (`[mem.str.empty]`) and not this module's choice:
/// there is no boundary anywhere, so there is nothing to cut. std guarded
/// this with a trap while the three lanes disagreed about it (F-0055,
/// closed at the sc11 pin); the guard is gone and the delegation is the
/// contract. No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var n = 0
/// for p in str.split("a,,b", ",") { n = n + 1 }
/// n = n + 0
/// n == 3
/// let whole = str.split("a,b", "")
/// whole.len == 1
/// whole[0] == "a,b"
/// ```
pub fn split(s: str, sep: str) -> List[str] {
    s.split(sep)
}

/// `split` stopping after `n` pieces: at most `n` are produced and the
/// LAST one keeps the whole unsplit remainder, separators and all. `n <= 0`
/// produces no pieces at all — the honest reading of "at most n" — and
/// `n == 1` is the whole string as one piece.
///
/// An empty `sep` yields the whole of `s` as one piece for any `n >= 1`,
/// which is `split`'s ruled answer truncated to at most `n`
/// (`[mem.str.empty]`) — the guard below states it explicitly because this
/// body walks `find` rather than delegating, and `find("")` is 0 forever.
/// Allocates the list; the pieces are slices of `s`. No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var n = 0
/// for p in str.splitn("a,b,c", ",", 2) { n = n + 1 }
/// n = n + 0
/// n == 2
/// let whole = str.splitn("a,b", "", 3)
/// whole.len == 1
/// whole[0] == "a,b"
/// ```
pub fn splitn(s: str, sep: str, n: int) -> List[str] {
    var out = List[str]()
    if sep.is_empty() {
        if n > 0 {
            (mut out).push(s)
        }
        return out
    }
    if n <= 0 {
        return out
    }
    var rest = s
    var left = n
    while left > 1 {
        let at = rest.find(sep) else 0 - 1
        if at < 0 {
            left = 1
        } else {
            (mut out).push(rest[0..at])
            rest = rest[at + sep.len..]
            left = left - 1
        }
    }
    (mut out).push(rest)
    out
}

/// `split`'s pieces in REVERSE order — last piece first. The pieces
/// themselves are not reversed and no byte moves: only the list order
/// differs, so `rsplit(s, sep)` and `split(s, sep)` always have the same
/// length and the same members.
///
/// An empty `sep` yields `split`'s one whole piece, in a list of one, where
/// order cannot matter (`[mem.str.empty]`). Allocates the list. No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var first = "?"
/// for p in str.rsplit("a,b,c", ",") { first = "{first}{p}" }
/// first == "?cba"
/// ```
pub fn rsplit(s: str, sep: str) -> List[str] {
    let parts = split(s, sep)
    let n = parts.len
    var out = List[str]()
    var i = n
    while i > 0 {
        (mut out).push(parts[i - 1])
        i = i - 1
    }
    out
}

/// The pieces of `s` before and after the FIRST occurrence of `sep`;
/// `none` when `sep` does not occur — the pair is only meaningful
/// together, so absence is one row and not two empty strings (§2).
///
/// The empty separator occurs at 0 (`find`'s contract), so
/// `split_once(s, "")` is `("", s)` rather than a trap: unlike `split`
/// there IS a well-defined answer, and this function's only obligation is
/// to agree with `find`. Both pieces are slices of `s`. No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let p = str.split_once("key=value=more", "=") else ("?", "?")
/// p.0 == "key"
/// p.1 == "value=more"
/// let q = str.split_once("nosep", "=") else ("?", "?")
/// q.0 == "?"
/// ```
pub fn split_once(s: str, sep: str) -> (str, str) ! {none} {
    let at = s.find(sep)?
    let head = s[0..at]
    let tail = s[at + sep.len..]
    (head, tail)
}

/// `split_once` at the LAST occurrence of `sep`: the pieces before and
/// after it, or `none` when it does not occur. The empty separator occurs
/// at `len(s)` (`rfind`'s contract), so `rsplit_once(s, "")` is `(s, "")`.
/// Both pieces are slices of `s`. No traps.
///
/// This is the extension-and-stem shape: `rsplit_once("archive.tar.gz",
/// ".")` is `("archive.tar", "gz")`, where `split_once` would have given
/// `("archive", "tar.gz")`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let p = str.rsplit_once("archive.tar.gz", ".") else ("?", "?")
/// p.0 == "archive.tar"
/// p.1 == "gz"
/// let q = str.rsplit_once("nodot", ".") else ("?", "?")
/// q.1 == "?"
/// ```
pub fn rsplit_once(s: str, sep: str) -> (str, str) ! {none} {
    let at = s.rfind(sep)?
    let head = s[0..at]
    let tail = s[at + sep.len..]
    (head, tail)
}

/// A fresh string with every NON-OVERLAPPING occurrence of `old` replaced
/// by `new`, left to right, allocated in the ambient region. Scanning
/// resumes AFTER each replacement, so `replace("aaa", "aa", "b")` is
/// `"ba"` and a `new` that contains `old` does not feed itself.
///
/// An empty `old` replaces NOTHING — the result is `s` — which is the
/// language's ruling (`[mem.str.empty]`): an empty pattern occurs
/// everywhere and at no width, so inserting `new` at every position would
/// be a different operation with a different name. std trapped here while
/// the lanes disagreed (F-0055, closed at the sc11 pin) and delegates now.
/// No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.replace("a,b,c", ",", ";") == "a;b;c"
/// str.replace("aaa", "aa", "b") == "ba"
/// str.replace("wolf", "z", "y") == "wolf"
/// str.replace("héllo", "l", "L") == "héLLo"
/// str.replace("a,b", "", "-") == "a,b"
/// ```
pub fn replace(s: str, old: str, new: str) -> str {
    s.replace(old, new)
}

/// `replace` stopping after `n` replacements; the rest of `s` is copied
/// unchanged. `n <= 0` replaces nothing and returns `s`.
///
/// An empty `old` is the identity for any `n`, which is `replace`'s ruled
/// answer (`[mem.str.empty]`) — stated as a guard because this body walks
/// `find`, and `find("")` is 0 forever. No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.replacen("a,b,c", ",", ";", 1) == "a;b,c"
/// str.replacen("a,b,c", ",", ";", 9) == "a;b;c"
/// str.replacen("a,b,c", ",", ";", 0) == "a,b,c"
/// str.replacen("a,b", "", "-", 2) == "a,b"
/// ```
pub fn replacen(s: str, old: str, new: str, n: int) -> str {
    if old.is_empty() {
        return s
    }
    var out = ""
    var rest = s
    var left = n
    while left > 0 {
        let at = rest.find(old) else 0 - 1
        if at < 0 {
            left = 0
        } else {
            let head = rest[0..at]
            out = "{out}{head}{new}"
            rest = rest[at + old.len..]
            left = left - 1
        }
    }
    "{out}{rest}"
}

/// `s` with every code point lowercased, freshly allocated in the ambient
/// region. Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// The mapping is the implementation's, and it is worth stating plainly:
/// the sprint contract planned ASCII-only case mapping here, but every
/// pinned implementation performs simple Unicode case mapping (`"ÉA"`
/// lowercases to `"éa"`). std delegates rather than reimplement, because
/// an in-library mapping would need the case tables nothing carries in
/// wolf source yet — so this function's contract is "the implementation's
/// simple case mapping", and which mapping std should REQUIRE is the filed
/// unicode-tables decision (F-0018). Locale is never a parameter, in core,
/// ever.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.lower("WOLF") == "wolf"
/// str.lower("Wolf-Lang") == "wolf-lang"
/// str.lower("") == ""
/// ```
pub fn lower(s: str) -> str {
    s.lower()
}

/// `s` with every code point uppercased, freshly allocated in the ambient
/// region. The mapping story is `lower`'s, exactly. Total: no error row,
/// no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.upper("wolf") == "WOLF"
/// str.upper("Wolf-Lang") == "WOLF-LANG"
/// str.upper("") == ""
/// ```
pub fn upper(s: str) -> str {
    s.upper()
}

/// Whether `a` and `b` are equal ignoring case, by comparing their
/// case-folded forms. Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// The sprint contract names this "ASCII, named honestly". The honest name
/// at this pin drops the ASCII: the folding is `lower`'s, which every
/// pinned implementation performs over all of Unicode. Callers get
/// case-insensitive comparison as strong as the implementation's `lower` —
/// never weaker, and never locale-sensitive.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.eq_ignore_case("WOLF", "wolf") == true
/// str.eq_ignore_case("Wolf", "wolves") == false
/// str.eq_ignore_case("", "") == true
/// ```
pub fn eq_ignore_case(a: str, b: str) -> bool {
    a.lower() == b.lower()
}

/// `s` repeated `n` times, freshly allocated. `n == 0` gives the empty
/// string. A NEGATIVE `n` traps `assert` — the language's own fault, from
/// the builtin, on all three lanes: a repeat count cannot be negative, and
/// s71 RULED the KIND (`[mem.str.repeat]`, wolf-lang#57) to be the caller
/// contract violation it is rather than the `bounds` the runtime used to
/// report. std does not guard it, because a trap is the right answer for a
/// violation the caller could have checked (§2) and because a guard would
/// hide the one place the implementations agree. Twice re-measured, twice
/// changed: sc03 read `""` from the interpreter, sc09 measured
/// `trap(bounds)` on every lane, sc11 measures `trap(assert)` on every lane
/// — which is exactly why the claim lives in
/// `tests/str/repeat_negative_trap.lu` and not only in this sentence
/// (F-0056, closed).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.repeat("ab", 3) == "ababab"
/// str.repeat("ab", 1) == "ab"
/// str.repeat("ab", 0) == ""
/// ```
pub fn repeat(s: str, n: int) -> str {
    s.repeat(n)
}

/// `s` with leading and trailing whitespace removed — a slice of `s`, no
/// copy. Whitespace is Unicode `White_Space` (the builtin's set, pinned
/// code point by code point by `tests/str/trim_whitespace_set.lu`). Total:
/// no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.trim("  wolf  ") == "wolf"
/// str.trim("\n\twolf\r\n") == "wolf"
/// str.trim("   ") == ""
/// ```
pub fn trim(s: str) -> str {
    s.trim()
}

/// `s` with LEADING whitespace removed — a slice of `s`, no copy. Same
/// whitespace set as `trim`, and the equality of the two sets is a test,
/// not a hope (§9). Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// This body was 95 lines until sc09: a `White_Space` table written as
/// `starts_with` probes, because sc03 had no way to scan a string it did
/// not already know the shape of. s37's builtin `trim_start` replaced the
/// table with a delegation, and the whitespace-set test outlived it — what
/// it pins now is the BUILTIN's set, which is what it was always really
/// checking.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.trim_start("  wolf  ") == "wolf  "
/// str.trim_start("wolf") == "wolf"
/// str.trim_start("   ") == ""
/// ```
pub fn trim_start(s: str) -> str {
    s.trim_start()
}

/// `s` with TRAILING whitespace removed — a slice of `s`, no copy. Same
/// whitespace set as `trim`. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.trim_end("  wolf  ") == "  wolf"
/// str.trim_end("wolf") == "wolf"
/// str.trim_end("   ") == ""
/// ```
pub fn trim_end(s: str) -> str {
    s.trim_end()
}

/// The lines of `s`, in order, each without its terminator. A trailing
/// newline does NOT produce a final empty line, and the empty string has
/// no lines at all. `\r\n` counts as one terminator and the `\r` is not
/// part of the line. Allocates the list (`to_`-grade allocation, §1);
/// the lines themselves are slices of `s`. Total: no error row, no traps
/// beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var seen = 0
/// for line in str.lines("a\nb\n") { seen = seen + 1 }
/// seen = seen + 0
/// seen == 2
/// ```
pub fn lines(s: str) -> List[str] {
    s.lines()
}

/// The whitespace-separated words of `s`, in order, dropping empty runs:
/// any run of whitespace separates, leading and trailing whitespace
/// produce no empty words, and a string of only whitespace has no words.
/// Whitespace is `trim`'s set. Allocates the list; the words are slices of
/// `s`. Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var seen = 0
/// for word in str.words("  the   wolf  ") { seen = seen + 1 }
/// seen = seen + 0
/// seen == 2
/// ```
pub fn words(s: str) -> List[str] {
    s.words()
}

/// Walks the whitespace-separated words of `s` in order and calls `f`
/// once per word, building nothing: the walk is the compiler's own lazy
/// `for`-head over the receiver's bytes (`[mem.str.view]`, s84), so
/// each word is a zero-copy slice of `s` and no list exists. `words` is
/// this walk with a `List[str]` at the end; when the list itself is not
/// wanted, this is the cheaper spelling. Same word rule as `words`:
/// whitespace is `trim`'s set, empty runs separate, no word is empty —
/// a string of only whitespace calls `f` zero times.
///
/// The example is prose because a doc example can neither define nor
/// import a fn value (F-0085/F-0086; the sc13 convention): with
/// `fn shout(w: str) -> () { print("{w}") }` at top level,
/// `str.each_word("  the wolf  ", shout)` prints `the` then `wolf`.
/// `tests/str/each_word_walk.lu` is the measured witness — including
/// the native-lane disassembly proving the walk fuses in std's own
/// module context (no `__wolf_rt_str_split`, the inline predicate).
pub fn each_word(s: str, f: fn(str) -> ()) -> () {
    for w in s.words() { f(w) }
}

/// The number of whitespace-separated words in `s` — `words` without
/// the list OR the callback: the same fused walk as `each_word`, folded
/// to a count. This fold earns its own name because the callable tier
/// cannot express it: a fn value captures nothing (F-0085/F-0086), so
/// counting through `each_word` has nowhere to put the counter. Word
/// rule as ever: whitespace is `trim`'s set, no word is empty — a
/// whitespace-only or empty string counts zero.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.words_count("  the   wolf  runs ") == 3
/// ```
pub fn words_count(s: str) -> int {
    var n = 0
    for w in s.words() { n = n + 1 }
    n
}

/// `parts` concatenated with the separator `sep` between consecutive
/// items — the separator is the RECEIVER, as the sprint contract has it,
/// which is why `sep` comes first. No separator is added before the first
/// or after the last part; an empty list joins to the empty string and a
/// one-item list to that item. Allocates in the ambient region. Total: no
/// error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// `join` and `split` are inverses whenever no part contains `sep`:
/// `join(sep, split(s, sep)) == s` for every `s` and every non-empty
/// `sep`, which `tests/str/split_join_round_trip.lu` holds them to.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var parts = List[str]()
/// (mut parts).push("the")
/// (mut parts).push("wolf")
/// str.join(" ", parts) == "the wolf"
/// str.join("", parts) == "thewolf"
/// ```
pub fn join(sep: str, parts: List[str]) -> str {
    let n = parts.len
    var out = ""
    var i = 0
    while i < n {
        let part = parts[i]
        if i == 0 {
            out = "{part}"
        } else {
            out = "{out}{sep}{part}"
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    out
}

/// The number of CODE POINTS in `s` — the count `len` deliberately does
/// not give (§9: a function that counts code points says so in its name).
/// O(bytes), and equal to `len(s)` exactly when `s` is ASCII.
///
/// Counted by the UTF-8 invariant rather than by decoding: a lead byte is
/// anything outside 128..191, so the count is the number of non-
/// continuation bytes and no arithmetic can overflow. Total: no error row,
/// no traps.
///
/// The walk consumes the byte view where it stands (`for b in s.bytes()`)
/// rather than binding it first. s77 made that the difference between a
/// view and a copy: `bytes()` is the receiver's own pointer and length in
/// a consuming position, and a `let` binding is what materializes it. The
/// body sc09 wrote bound `bs` and paid a list for every string it counted.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.char_count("wolf") == 4
/// str.char_count("héllo") == 5
/// str.char_count("é🐺") == 2
/// str.char_count("") == 0
/// ```
pub fn char_count(s: str) -> int {
    var seen = 0
    for b in s.bytes() {
        if b < 128 || b > 191 {
            seen = seen + 1
        }
    }
    seen
}

/// The byte offset at which each code point of `s` STARTS, in order — the
/// offset half of the blocked `chars()` (see the module header), and every
/// offset in it is a boundary `get` will accept. `len(s)` is deliberately
/// NOT included: this lists code points, and there is none at the end.
///
/// Allocates the OUTPUT list and nothing else. The offset is carried in a
/// counter beside a `for` over the byte view rather than driving an index
/// INTO it. That spelling was a lane decision when it was written — indexing
/// a view was refused by wolfc's checked tier (F-0071, closed at the sc12
/// pin) — and it is kept now because it is the better body: one pass, one
/// counter, no list. The body sc09 wrote bound `bs` first and copied every
/// byte of `s` before reading one of them.
///
/// Total: no error row, no traps beyond the output list's allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var last = 0 - 1
/// for at in str.char_offsets("héllo") { last = at }
/// last == 5
/// ```
pub fn char_offsets(s: str) -> List[int] {
    var out = List[int]()
    var at = 0
    for b in s.bytes() {
        if b < 128 || b > 191 {
            (mut out).push(at)
        }
        at = at + 1
    }
    out
}

/// The Unicode SCALAR VALUES of `s`'s code points, in order — the interim
/// face of the blocked `to_list_chars` (module header), in the `int`
/// currency `std.unicode` already spells a code point with. Feed them to
/// `std.unicode`'s classifiers: `unicode.is_ascii_digit(c)` over this list
/// is the character classification a scanner wants, and it is the reason
/// this function exists rather than waiting for `char`.
///
/// Total over every `str`: a `str` is valid UTF-8 by construction (D24), so
/// the decode below has no error path and needs no validation — the
/// version of it that DOES validate is `std.bytes.is_utf8`, over bytes
/// nobody promised anything about. Allocates the OUTPUT list and nothing
/// else.
///
/// **The decoder is a one-pass state machine now, and that is the shape the
/// byte view asks for.** sc09 wrote it as a random-access walk — bind the
/// list, read `bs[i + 1]`, `bs[i + 2]`, `bs[i + 3]`, advance `i` by the
/// width — which needed a materialized copy to index into. s77 makes
/// iteration the free position, so the lead byte now sets a PENDING count
/// and each continuation folds six bits into the accumulator. The two forms
/// agree byte for byte; this one reads `s`'s own storage and never builds a
/// list to read it from, and it is shorter — which is why it stayed after
/// F-0071 (the checked tier's refusal of an indexed view) closed at the
/// sc12 pin.
///
/// The continuation count is trusted rather than checked, and that is the
/// UTF-8 invariant rather than an oversight: a lead byte in a valid `str`
/// is followed by exactly as many continuations as it announces.
/// `std.bytes.is_utf8` is where that assumption is checked, because there
/// the bytes are a caller's and not the language's.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var sum = 0
/// for c in str.code_points("aé") { sum = sum + c }
/// sum = sum + 0
/// sum == 330
/// ```
pub fn code_points(s: str) -> List[int] {
    var out = List[int]()
    var acc = 0
    var pending = 0
    for b in s.bytes() {
        if pending > 0 {
            acc = acc * 64 + (b - 128)
            pending = pending - 1
            if pending == 0 {
                (mut out).push(acc)
            }
        } else if b < 128 {
            (mut out).push(b)
        } else if b < 224 {
            acc = b - 192
            pending = 1
        } else if b < 240 {
            acc = b - 224
            pending = 2
        } else {
            acc = b - 240
            pending = 3
        }
    }
    out
}

/// Whether every code point in `s` is ASCII (below U+0080) — the cheap
/// question a caller asks before treating byte offsets as character
/// positions, and the one classification that needs no tables at all.
/// The empty string is ASCII. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// `is_ascii(s)` is exactly `char_count(s) == len(s)`, and it is a
/// function rather than that expression because the equality is a fact
/// about UTF-8 the caller should not have to know.
///
/// It is also the cheapest function in this module to get wrong the
/// expensive way: until sc12 it copied every byte of `s` into a list and
/// then returned `false` at the first one above 127. It walks the view now
/// and stops there — a `return` out of a `for` over `s.bytes()` runs on all
/// three lanes.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// str.is_ascii("wolf-lang") == true
/// str.is_ascii("héllo") == false
/// str.is_ascii("") == true
/// ```
pub fn is_ascii(s: str) -> bool {
    for b in s.bytes() {
        if b > 127 {
            return false
        }
    }
    true
}

/// `s` copied into a fresh `std.strbuf.StrBuf` — the allocating
/// conversion into the builder (`to_`, §1: it allocates; `strbuf.to_str`
/// is the borrowing direction back). Total: no error row, no traps beyond
/// allocation.
///
/// No fenced example: asserting about the result needs `std.strbuf`, and
/// the extractor imports exactly the documented module (§4's one-module
/// note), so the worked example lives in
/// `tests/str/to_strbuf_round_trip.lu` instead. In prose: after
/// `let b = str.to_strbuf("wolf")`, `strbuf.len(b)` is 4 and
/// `strbuf.to_str(b)` is `"wolf"` — and appending to `b` leaves `s`
/// untouched, because `str` is immutable and this is a copy.
pub fn to_strbuf(s: str) -> strbuf.StrBuf {
    var b = strbuf.new()
    strbuf.push_str(mut b, s)
    b
}
